+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `starlark` (facebook/starlark-rust)
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2,
+dev-dependency of the `starlark` crate). Tests live in the crate's existing test
+files:
+
+- `starlark/src/tests/basic.rs` (`mod prop`) — value/arithmetic semantics
+- `starlark/src/tests/call.rs` (`mod prop`) — call-stack limit
+- `starlark/src/tests/uncategorized.rs` (`mod parse_prop`, `mod print_prop`,
+  `mod nesting_prop`, plus pinned bug tests) — parser and pretty-printer
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Twelve properties, each with the evidence that grounds it:
+
+1. **`test_prop_int_binop_matches_bigint_reference`** — every integer binary
+   operator (`+ - * // % & | ^`) agrees with an independent `num_bigint`
+   reference (floored `//`/`%` implemented separately in the test from
+   truncated division). Inputs mix `i128`-range values, explicit boundary
+   values around the inline-`i32`/`i64`/`u64` representation limits, and
+   60-digit integers.
+   *Evidence:* `docs/types.md` / crate docs state ints are arbitrary
+   precision; `bitwise_test` and `arithmetic_test` in `basic.rs` pin
+   hand-picked instances of the same semantics.
+2. **`test_prop_int_shift_matches_bigint_reference`** — `<<`/`>>` by 0–512
+   bits agree with `num_bigint` (including floor semantics of `>>` on
+   negative values).
+   *Evidence:* `bitwise_test` cases like `-1 << 2 == -4`.
+3. **`test_prop_int_comparison_matches_bigint_reference`** — `< <= > >= == !=`
+   agree with `BigInt`'s `Ord`, with equality force-generated half the time.
+   *Evidence:* comparison cases in `basic.rs::test_comparison`.
+4. **`test_prop_nested_int_arithmetic_matches_bigint_reference`** — a random
+   nested `+/-/*` expression tree evaluates to the value computed by an
+   independent BigInt evaluator over the same tree. Leaves are randomly
+   wrapped in `noop(...)` so both the constant-folding and runtime bytecode
+   paths are exercised.
+   *Evidence:* same as (1) plus the `noop` idiom used throughout the
+   existing tests to defeat optimization.
+5. **`test_prop_sorted_matches_rust_sort`** — `sorted(list_of_ints)` equals
+   Rust's `slice::sort` result.
+   *Evidence:* stdlib `sorted` documentation.
+6. **`test_prop_string_repr_parse_eval_roundtrip`** — for arbitrary Unicode
+   strings `s`, the literal produced by `StarlarkStr::repr(s)` (the same code
+   as the `repr()` builtin) parses through the independent lexer path and
+   evaluates back to exactly `s`.
+   *Evidence:* `StarlarkStr::repr` doc "Format a Rust string like `repr(s)`";
+   lexer string-escape support.
+7. **`test_prop_string_len_matches_char_count`** — `len(s)` equals Rust's
+   `s.chars().count()`.
+   *Evidence:* `StarlarkStr::length` uses `fast_string::len` (char count).
+8. **`test_prop_equal_dict_keys_collide`** — eq/hash contract via dicts:
+   inserting two random hashable values (`None`, bools, small and 30-digit
+   ints, int-/half-valued floats including `-0.0`, short strings, nested
+   tuples) as keys leaves one entry iff `x == y`. Catches cross-type
+   eq-without-hash bugs (`1` vs `1.0` vs big-int `1`, `0` vs `-0.0`).
+   *Evidence:* `pointer_i32.rs` and `float.rs` both hash through
+   `NumRef::get_hash_64` precisely to satisfy this contract.
+9. **`test_prop_parse_never_panics`** — `AstModule::parse` returns `Err`
+   rather than panicking on arbitrary Unicode text and on Starlark-shaped
+   token soup, in all three dialects, and the error value can be
+   Debug-formatted.
+   *Evidence:* `parse` returns `crate::Result`; the existing
+   `test_fuzzer_59102/59371/59839` tests pin past oss-fuzz panics of exactly
+   this shape (including format-only panics).
+10. **`test_prop_parse_print_reparse_roundtrip`** — for generated well-formed
+    modules (expressions incl. all binary/unary ops, lists, tuples, dicts,
+    conditionals, calls with `*`/`**`, indexing, comprehensions, lambdas;
+    statements incl. assignments, augmented assignments, type-annotated
+    assignment, `if`/`else`, `for`, `def` with parameter shapes, `load`),
+    the `Display` form of the parsed AST re-parses successfully and prints
+    identically the second time (print∘parse idempotence).
+    *Evidence:* `Display for Stmt/Expr` in `starlark_syntax/src/syntax/ast.rs`
+    prints concrete syntax. Slices, attribute access on literals, and
+    f-strings are excluded — each is a **known failure** pinned by a
+    deterministic test (see Suspected bugs).
+11. **`test_prop_unbounded_recursion_errors_gracefully`** — a cycle of 1–20
+    Starlark functions (varying call shapes: plain call, through arithmetic,
+    through a comprehension), optionally with a drawn
+    `set_max_callstack_size(1..=200)`, always fails with the documented
+    "Starlark call stack overflow" error instead of aborting the process.
+    *Evidence:* `funcall_test`'s `rec1`/`rec2` cases; error text in
+    `cheap_call_stack.rs`; `Evaluator::set_max_callstack_size` docs.
+12. **`test_prop_moderately_nested_expressions_evaluate`** — nested parens,
+    lists, unary-minus chains, and `+`-chains up to depth 40 parse and
+    evaluate to the expected value. The bound protects the test process from
+    the process-aborting stack overflows documented below, not a library
+    contract.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test -p starlark --lib`: **950 passed, 3 failed, 2 ignored**.
+  - The 3 failures are the intentionally kept KNOWN FAILURE tests pinning
+    real pretty-printer bugs (below). They are deterministic plain `#[test]`s
+    (no randomness), so they fail identically on every run.
+  - The 2 ignored tests are minimal reproducers for **process-aborting**
+    stack overflows (SIGABRT); running them kills the test process, so per
+    policy they are `#[ignore]`d with explanatory messages.
+- All 12 hegel properties pass at the default 100 cases, and also passed an
+  exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` each, ~12,000 total cases,
+  temporarily set and reverted).
+- Liveness verified: deliberately corrupting the reference in property (1)
+  produced a fully shrunk counterexample (`a = 0, op = "+", b = 0`); a
+  generator mistake in property (10) (a defaulted parameter emitted after
+  `**kwargs`) was caught and shrunk by hegel during development.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+All in this checkout at `dd23c83`. None were fixed, per instructions.
+
+1. **`Display` for `Expr::Slice` emits garbage** (pinned:
+   `tests::uncategorized::test_slice_display_reparses`, failing).
+   Minimal counterexample: `x[1:2]` pretty-prints as `x[]1:2` (not valid
+   Starlark; also drops any closing bracket).
+   Code path: `starlark_syntax/src/syntax/ast.rs`, `impl Display for Expr`,
+   `Expr::Slice` arm — `write!(f, "{}[]", e.node)?` writes both brackets
+   before the indices. Affects every error/debug message that prints a slice.
+2. **`Display` for `Expr::FString` prints the format string unquoted**
+   (pinned: `test_fstring_display_reparses`, failing).
+   Minimal counterexample: `f"a{x}b"` prints as `a{}b.format(x)` instead of
+   `"a{}b".format(x)`.
+   Code path: same file, `Expr::FString` arm — `write!(f, "{}.format(",
+   x.format.node)` prints the raw `String` without `fmt_string_literal`.
+3. **`Display` for `Expr::Dot` does not parenthesize integer-literal bases**
+   (pinned: `test_dot_on_int_literal_display_reparses`, failing).
+   Minimal counterexample: `(1).imag` prints as `1.imag`, which re-lexes as
+   float `1.` + identifier and no longer parses.
+4. **Process abort: parser stack overflow on deep parenthesis nesting**
+   (reproducer kept `#[ignore]`d:
+   `test_parser_stack_overflow_on_deeply_nested_parens`).
+   `AstModule::parse` on `"(" * 200 + "0" + ")" * 200` dies with
+   `fatal runtime error: stack overflow` (SIGABRT) in a debug build.
+   Code path: the recursive-descent parser
+   (`starlark_syntax/src/syntax/parser_rd.rs`) has no recursion-depth guard.
+5. **Process abort: compile/eval stack overflow on ASTs that parsed fine**
+   (reproducer kept `#[ignore]`d:
+   `test_eval_stack_overflow_on_deeply_nested_unary`).
+   `-` repeated ~100 times before `0` (also `[[[…]]]` at depth ~100) parses
+   successfully (parser handles 1000+), then `Evaluator::eval_module`
+   aborts the process. The interpreter's own "Starlark call stack overflow"
+   guard does not apply — it counts Starlark frames, not AST/compiler
+   recursion. Measured thresholds are debug-build; release goes deeper but
+   is still unguarded.
+6. **Minor, not pinned: float literal `Display` is lossy.** `1.0`, `5.`, and
+   `1e10` print as `1`, `5`, `10000000000` (Rust `{}` on `f64`), so the
+   printed program re-parses as an *int* literal — a semantic change. It is
+   print-idempotent, so property (10) tolerates it; flagged here because any
+   future "Display is reparseable source" use would silently change types
+   (and `5e-324` prints as a 330-character decimal).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"Pretty-print preserves semantics" (print → reparse → same values).**
+  Abandoned in favor of print-idempotence: float literals print as ints (bug
+  6), so the semantic version fails for a reason maintainers may consider
+  cosmetic (Display is only used in diagnostics). The idempotence form still
+  catches structural print bugs (and did — bugs 1–3).
+- **No other property had to be weakened.** Notably, int/float/bigint
+  cross-type hashing (property 8) and `>>` floor semantics on negative
+  bigints (property 2) — both plausible sources of unsoundness — turned out
+  to hold.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The workflow's "read the crate's own fuzz/regression tests first" advice
+  paid off directly: the `test_fuzzer_*` tests in `uncategorized.rs` showed
+  both where parse-robustness tests belong and the non-obvious trick that
+  some past panics only fire when *formatting* the parse error
+  (`format!("{res:?}")`), which the no-panic property now replicates.
+- The "process-aborting bugs" special case in Run and Reflect was essential
+  here (two SIGABRT stack overflows). One gap: the guidance assumes the
+  aborting input is *found by a hegel run*. Here uniform generation would
+  essentially never produce 200 nested parens, so the crashes were found by
+  a manual depth probe suggested by reading the parser for missing depth
+  guards. A sentence in the skill like "for recursive-input code, probe
+  depth limits directly with a parameterized loop before trusting a green
+  no-panic property" would generalize that.
+- `compose!`-returning-`BoxedGenerator` for recursive generators (from the
+  Rust reference's TOML example) worked exactly as documented, including the
+  `'static` lifetime spelling and the no-`move` rule. Recursive text
+  generators for grammars are common enough that a dedicated worked example
+  (statements + suites with indentation) might be worth adding.
+- Counterexample output printing each `let x = ...;` draw made the two
+  development-time failures (liveness check, `**kwargs` parameter-order
+  generator bug) immediately diagnosable; shrinking was fast (<1s) even for
+  the multi-draw program generator.
+- One friction point: there is no global case-count override (documented in
+  the reference), so the 10x exploratory run required editing 12 attributes
+  and reverting; a `HEGEL_TEST_CASES` env var would remove a manual step.
